WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.376 --> 00:00:04.421
- What's up Believe Nation?

00:00:04.421 --> 00:00:06.259
I started the MentorMe
series with the goal

00:00:06.259 --> 00:00:09.314
to try to learn from people
who've done a lot more than us,

00:00:09.314 --> 00:00:11.334
hang around them a little bit longer,

00:00:11.334 --> 00:00:13.105
and by being in their environment,

00:00:13.105 --> 00:00:15.233
hopefully some of their
mindsets, their beliefs,

00:00:15.233 --> 00:00:17.569
their way of thinking seep into us

00:00:17.569 --> 00:00:20.715
to help us become the
best version of ourselves.

00:00:20.715 --> 00:00:21.548
So today we're going to learn

00:00:21.548 --> 00:00:24.326
how to be unshakable with Tony Robbins.

00:00:24.326 --> 00:00:25.380
Mentor me, Tony.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.129
And as always guys, as
you're watchin' the clip,

00:00:27.129 --> 00:00:29.354
if you hear somethin' that
really, really resonates with you

00:00:29.354 --> 00:00:31.362
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:31.362 --> 00:00:33.847
and put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired

00:00:33.847 --> 00:00:35.440
and also when you write it down,

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.300
you're much more likely to
lock it in for yourself too.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.133
Enjoy.

00:00:40.586 --> 00:00:44.003
(light, uplifting music)

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:54.374
- Tony, the idea of being unshakeable

00:00:54.374 --> 00:00:56.924
is clearly more than
money, but in this context,

00:00:56.924 --> 00:00:59.168
what does it truly mean to be unshakeable?

00:00:59.168 --> 00:01:00.675
- It's a state of mind.

00:01:00.675 --> 00:01:02.543
It relates to money because most people,

00:01:02.543 --> 00:01:03.722
when they're trying to
figure out how to create

00:01:03.722 --> 00:01:06.906
financial freedom create
more stress for themselves

00:01:06.906 --> 00:01:08.551
worrying about what's going on.

00:01:08.551 --> 00:01:10.868
And when you're truly
unshakeable you have the mindset

00:01:10.868 --> 00:01:12.917
where you understand how the market works,

00:01:12.917 --> 00:01:15.562
you understand that real
estate can go up or down,

00:01:15.562 --> 00:01:18.875
stocks can go up or down, but
your family can still do well.

00:01:18.875 --> 00:01:21.060
Unshakeable is a bear market can come,

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.780
a correction can come, and
there's no fear in you,

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.051
or if there is a little
fear, we're all human,

00:01:26.051 --> 00:01:28.194
you don't stay there, you
find a way to break through,

00:01:28.194 --> 00:01:29.530
you find your center.

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:31.746
And when you have that
center, you do what's right

00:01:31.746 --> 00:01:33.900
to be able to take care of
yourself and your family.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:37.232
And I was interviewing
about a year and a half ago,

00:01:37.232 --> 00:01:39.718
you know, the former fed
chair, Alan Greenspan,

00:01:39.718 --> 00:01:42.236
who for 19 years, the most
powerful man in finance

00:01:42.236 --> 00:01:44.783
in the world, under four
different presidents.

00:01:44.783 --> 00:01:47.446
Spent five hours with
him, about three privately

00:01:47.446 --> 00:01:49.673
and two on stage in front of a group,

00:01:49.673 --> 00:01:51.031
and one of my final questions to him,

00:01:51.031 --> 00:01:52.880
and we talked about all this crazy change

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:54.465
and all this volatility, and how do people

00:01:54.465 --> 00:01:56.705
get things together, I
said, look if you were

00:01:56.705 --> 00:02:00.646
back head of the fed
today, what would you do?

00:02:00.646 --> 00:02:02.547
And there was this long pause, he paused,

00:02:02.547 --> 00:02:03.996
he paused and then he
leaned into me, he said,

00:02:03.996 --> 00:02:06.494
Tony, I'd resign (laughing).

00:02:06.494 --> 00:02:08.401
And that did not build
much confidence in me

00:02:08.401 --> 00:02:10.091
or anybody else who'd be watching.

00:02:10.091 --> 00:02:13.007
And yet, in the midst of
all that, as you well know,

00:02:13.007 --> 00:02:16.388
there's a few unicorns in the
financial market, very few.

00:02:16.388 --> 00:02:18.870
These people that have
found a way to do well

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.032
over all the decades, in
good times and in bad.

00:02:22.032 --> 00:02:24.612
And they know what it
takes to be unshakeable.

00:02:24.612 --> 00:02:27.043
And it isn't just guts or confidence,

00:02:27.043 --> 00:02:30.364
it's understanding certain
facts that when you know them,

00:02:30.364 --> 00:02:31.239
they free you.

00:02:31.239 --> 00:02:33.123
The metaphor I give
people is that, you know,

00:02:33.123 --> 00:02:36.247
if you go, and most of us have
heard the old Sufi metaphor

00:02:36.247 --> 00:02:38.476
of, you know, the man is walking along

00:02:38.476 --> 00:02:40.475
in the middle of the
night and he sees a snake

00:02:40.475 --> 00:02:42.060
and freaks out, runs away.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.060
And he comes in in the
morning and what does he see?

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:45.325
It was a rope.

00:02:45.325 --> 00:02:47.333
And so once you know it's a rope,

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:48.647
you can be there in
the middle of the night

00:02:48.647 --> 00:02:49.929
and you're not scared again.

00:02:49.929 --> 00:02:51.680
This book, Unshakeable, is this,

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.467
it's really a financial
freedom playbook for anybody.

00:02:54.467 --> 00:02:56.973
It's the real essentials about
how to go from where you are

00:02:56.973 --> 00:02:58.489
to where you really want to be,

00:02:58.489 --> 00:03:00.758
but how to do it with peace of mind.

00:03:00.758 --> 00:03:02.216
How to do it even when things are volatile

00:03:02.216 --> 00:03:03.508
and enjoy yourself.

00:03:03.508 --> 00:03:05.296
I wanted to write a book
that would protect people

00:03:05.296 --> 00:03:07.704
because we all know a crash is coming.

00:03:07.704 --> 00:03:08.980
And if you're not in the market,

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.114
you're still going to experience it

00:03:10.114 --> 00:03:11.343
if you're not prepared for it,

00:03:11.343 --> 00:03:13.358
because it effects the economy as a whole.

00:03:13.358 --> 00:03:16.016
And every five years we have, on average,

00:03:16.016 --> 00:03:18.031
we have a bear market or a crash.

00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:21.054
And it lasts a year, and it drops 33%.

00:03:21.054 --> 00:03:23.212
But every single one of those,

00:03:23.212 --> 00:03:25.371
you don't lose any money unless you sell.

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:27.257
And it's really important for
Americans to understand this

00:03:27.257 --> 00:03:30.211
because America's unique,
in 2 1/2 centuries,

00:03:30.211 --> 00:03:32.966
every crash is followed immediately by

00:03:32.966 --> 00:03:34.316
an explosion up and a bull market.

00:03:34.316 --> 00:03:37.416
So you all remember 2008, you
remember the market dropped

00:03:37.416 --> 00:03:40.562
almost 50% from peak to
trough if you're aware of it.

00:03:40.562 --> 00:03:43.793
But from March 9th, what,
a day or two from now,

00:03:43.793 --> 00:03:46.658
is the celebration of it, it
went up in the next 12 months

00:03:46.658 --> 00:03:51.329
69% and it's been going
up ever since for 250%.

00:03:51.329 --> 00:03:53.641
So, you get really wealthy
when those numbers change.

00:03:53.641 --> 00:03:55.837
So what I want to do is
free people from the fear,

00:03:55.837 --> 00:03:59.246
not by enthusiasm or positive
thinking but by facts.

00:03:59.246 --> 00:04:01.505
The facts are, there's
a correction every year.

00:04:01.505 --> 00:04:03.200
You shouldn't be surprised
when the market corrects.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:04.695
Last January was the worst opening

00:04:04.695 --> 00:04:06.282
in the history of the stock market,

00:04:06.282 --> 00:04:07.836
and we had a record-breaking year.

00:04:07.836 --> 00:04:10.347
People lost 2.2 trillion if they sold,

00:04:10.347 --> 00:04:11.788
but if they stayed they went nowhere.

00:04:11.788 --> 00:04:14.354
So when you know that every single year

00:04:14.354 --> 00:04:16.413
there's going to be a correction,
now a correction by the way

00:04:16.413 --> 00:04:20.356
means it drops 10 up to 20% from the peak.

00:04:20.356 --> 00:04:23.262
A bear market or crash is 20% or more.

00:04:23.262 --> 00:04:26.326
But every year since 1900, for 116 years,

00:04:26.326 --> 00:04:27.526
we've had a correction, it's like winter,

00:04:27.526 --> 00:04:28.869
you're not surprised when winter comes.

00:04:28.869 --> 00:04:31.379
Some are long, some are short,
but you know it's coming.

00:04:31.379 --> 00:04:32.567
- You've even got a
chapter in the book called

00:04:32.567 --> 00:04:33.616
Winter is Coming.

00:04:33.616 --> 00:04:34.974
- Yes, yes.
(audience laughing)

00:04:34.974 --> 00:04:37.364
It's my HBO special (laughing).

00:04:37.364 --> 00:04:39.902
But the reason I say that
is if you know it's coming,

00:04:39.902 --> 00:04:41.402
we want to prepare so you're protected,

00:04:41.402 --> 00:04:42.744
but also, for everyone listening,

00:04:42.744 --> 00:04:45.394
everyone who's joining us
online, think about this,

00:04:45.394 --> 00:04:46.857
if you're a millennial, for example,

00:04:46.857 --> 00:04:48.294
you got all this debt from college,

00:04:48.294 --> 00:04:50.309
it's so imbalanced right now,

00:04:50.309 --> 00:04:51.751
and you think you're
never going to get free,

00:04:51.751 --> 00:04:53.969
or you're a baby boomer
and you never got started

00:04:53.969 --> 00:04:56.638
and you think it's too
late, believe it or not,

00:04:56.638 --> 00:04:59.778
the crash, the next crash,
is the greatest opportunity

00:04:59.778 --> 00:05:02.307
in your life to leapfrog
from where you are,

00:05:02.307 --> 00:05:03.689
where you don't want to be financially,

00:05:03.689 --> 00:05:05.840
to where you want to be,
'cause everything goes on sale.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:07.855
Listen, the ladies in this room,

00:05:07.855 --> 00:05:11.817
if your favorite outfits or
shoes are on sale for 50% off

00:05:11.817 --> 00:05:13.053
you're pretty damn excited, right?

00:05:13.053 --> 00:05:14.043
(audience laughing)

00:05:14.043 --> 00:05:16.146
Well guys, if I said to you,
what's your favorite car,

00:05:16.146 --> 00:05:18.387
and you said a Ferrari and I
said you get it for 50% off,

00:05:18.387 --> 00:05:19.656
guys'd be out of their mind.

00:05:19.656 --> 00:05:22.350
But when the stock market
drops 50%, it's on sale,

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:23.308
most people freak out.

00:05:23.308 --> 00:05:25.369
Not Warren Buffet, not people like that.

00:05:25.369 --> 00:05:26.553
I interviewed a guy named Howard Marks,

00:05:26.553 --> 00:05:31.253
he was investing a billion
dollars a week in 2008 and 9,

00:05:31.253 --> 00:05:32.515
when the market was through the floor.

00:05:32.515 --> 00:05:34.605
And now he's worth a
hundred billion dollars

00:05:34.605 --> 00:05:36.326
'cause he bought everything for pennies.

00:05:36.326 --> 00:05:37.650
You got to think of it this way,

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:39.394
when things are goin' really great,

00:05:39.394 --> 00:05:41.488
what do people want for
the price of their home,

00:05:41.488 --> 00:05:44.219
or the price of their
stock, or their business?

00:05:44.219 --> 00:05:46.172
Its real value, or more?

00:05:46.172 --> 00:05:47.599
When it's going up like crazy,

00:05:47.599 --> 00:05:49.205
everybody wants more than it's worth.

00:05:49.205 --> 00:05:51.584
When it starts crashing
what will people take

00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:54.098
for their car, for their
business, for their stocks,

00:05:54.098 --> 00:05:54.989
what will they take?

00:05:54.989 --> 00:05:55.822
- [Audience] Anything.

00:05:55.822 --> 00:05:56.655
- Anything they can get.

00:05:56.655 --> 00:05:58.729
And so those opportunities
are the greatest ones

00:05:58.729 --> 00:06:00.270
in your life, and here's
what you got to know,

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:02.293
if you're stressed by
worrying about the market

00:06:02.293 --> 00:06:04.375
or the economy, there's
a correction every year,

00:06:04.375 --> 00:06:05.605
you're going to be stressed forever.

00:06:05.605 --> 00:06:07.815
If you're 35 and you're
going to live to 85,

00:06:07.815 --> 00:06:10.352
you got 50 more corrections to go through,

00:06:10.352 --> 00:06:13.391
and you know, a bear market
comes every five years,

00:06:13.391 --> 00:06:15.318
so you're going to have 10
more bear markets in your life.

00:06:15.318 --> 00:06:17.934
Time to stop being stressed,
time to become unshakeable,

00:06:17.934 --> 00:06:21.467
which simply means you have a
plan, you know what's going on

00:06:21.467 --> 00:06:24.064
you're not fearful 'cause you
know how this works long term

00:06:24.064 --> 00:06:25.513
and you're not going to
be taken advantage of,

00:06:25.513 --> 00:06:27.178
you're going to take
advantage of the environment.

00:06:27.178 --> 00:06:28.501
The goal if you own a business,

00:06:28.501 --> 00:06:30.894
and I would assume a lot of
your viewers are business owners

00:06:30.894 --> 00:06:32.856
or are getting started in business.

00:06:32.856 --> 00:06:35.637
No matter how good you are in
business, think about this,

00:06:35.637 --> 00:06:38.893
the one universal rule that
idiots in finance know,

00:06:38.893 --> 00:06:40.659
is diversification, it's
the only free lunch,

00:06:40.659 --> 00:06:42.221
you got to diversify.

00:06:42.221 --> 00:06:44.317
Because if you put all
your eggs in one basket,

00:06:44.317 --> 00:06:46.055
no matter how good the basket is,

00:06:46.055 --> 00:06:48.633
one day that real estate
market, that stock market,

00:06:48.633 --> 00:06:50.416
that bond market, that
collectables market,

00:06:50.416 --> 00:06:53.568
whatever you invest in, Ray
Dalio showed me statistically

00:06:53.568 --> 00:06:56.485
it'll drop 50 to 70% on a day.

00:06:57.337 --> 00:06:59.344
Now, if you're later in
life when that happens,

00:06:59.344 --> 00:07:00.461
it's over for you.

00:07:00.461 --> 00:07:03.030
So you have to diversify
and yet most people

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:04.612
they know real estate, so they do it,

00:07:04.612 --> 00:07:05.888
or they know stocks so they do it,

00:07:05.888 --> 00:07:08.122
or they grew up with their
parents flipping things.

00:07:08.122 --> 00:07:09.612
And it's the wrong thing to do.

00:07:09.612 --> 00:07:12.095
So you've got to diversify in
order for you to be able to

00:07:12.095 --> 00:07:15.841
truly succeed, and that's
why when you own a business,

00:07:15.841 --> 00:07:18.270
if you put all your
money in your business,

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.322
which is what most of do naturally,

00:07:20.322 --> 00:07:21.815
perhaps you see a lot.
- A lot of risk.

00:07:21.815 --> 00:07:23.428
- You put all your eggs in one basket

00:07:23.428 --> 00:07:25.632
and there's things that
can happen, I mean,

00:07:25.632 --> 00:07:28.157
you know, let's say you spend 20 years

00:07:28.157 --> 00:07:31.387
and you figured out how to
put together the ultimate map.

00:07:31.387 --> 00:07:33.416
And you remember Garmin
came out with this thing

00:07:33.416 --> 00:07:34.951
called a tomtom, I don't
know if you remember,

00:07:34.951 --> 00:07:35.864
you used to put on your,

00:07:35.864 --> 00:07:36.746
are you old enough to remember that?

00:07:36.746 --> 00:07:37.985
You could put on your phone.
- Of course, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:37.985 --> 00:07:40.211
- Or you just put on your dash.

00:07:40.211 --> 00:07:43.229
Cost a hundred bucks,
it was a breakthrough.

00:07:43.229 --> 00:07:44.062
- [Lewis] They were makin' like

00:07:44.062 --> 00:07:44.895
a hundred million or something, or yeah--

00:07:44.895 --> 00:07:47.944
- They were, six months
later, what happened?

00:07:47.944 --> 00:07:49.842
- [Lewis] The iPhone came
out with Google Maps.

00:07:49.842 --> 00:07:52.296
- That's right, these little
bastards, excuse my French,

00:07:52.296 --> 00:07:53.661
came out with it, put Google Maps,

00:07:53.661 --> 00:07:55.745
put their own map on here,
and it cost how much?

00:07:55.745 --> 00:07:56.578
Zero.

00:07:56.578 --> 00:07:57.546
What's that going to do to your business

00:07:57.546 --> 00:07:59.214
when someone takes your product or service

00:07:59.214 --> 00:08:00.720
and gives it away for free?

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:03.455
So I always tell people,
competition happens,

00:08:03.455 --> 00:08:05.847
technology happens, what you must do

00:08:05.847 --> 00:08:09.066
is have a second business
with no moving parts,

00:08:09.066 --> 00:08:12.237
no people, no time, maybe it takes you

00:08:12.237 --> 00:08:14.284
two, three days a year
for two or three hours

00:08:14.284 --> 00:08:15.481
after you've read the book.

00:08:15.481 --> 00:08:16.752
You put it in place and you measure it

00:08:16.752 --> 00:08:18.311
two or three times a year, that's it.

00:08:18.311 --> 00:08:19.337
Go on with your life.

00:08:19.337 --> 00:08:20.499
Now if there's a trouble in your business,

00:08:20.499 --> 00:08:21.850
you're financially set.

00:08:21.850 --> 00:08:24.756
I, in my life, have 31 companies now.

00:08:24.756 --> 00:08:27.816
We have, what do we have, 1200 employees,

00:08:27.816 --> 00:08:31.638
7 different industries, we
do five billion in sales.

00:08:31.638 --> 00:08:34.461
I mean I, that used to be
me and my seminar business.

00:08:34.461 --> 00:08:36.166
It's grown geometrically.

00:08:36.166 --> 00:08:37.645
But with all those moving parts,

00:08:37.645 --> 00:08:39.079
the only way I've been able to succeed

00:08:39.079 --> 00:08:41.147
is because I've taken
everyone of those businesses

00:08:41.147 --> 00:08:43.177
and I've diversified my assets so that

00:08:43.177 --> 00:08:46.089
when things were in trouble,
I still had enough economics

00:08:46.089 --> 00:08:48.293
to take care of myself and
keep the business going.

00:08:48.293 --> 00:08:51.493
So, everybody needs to
create a money machine,

00:08:51.493 --> 00:08:54.176
that works while you sleep,
that doesn't have moving parts,

00:08:54.176 --> 00:08:55.527
and that's what this is really about.

00:08:55.527 --> 00:08:59.032
- Can you talk about what it
means to discuss the market

00:08:59.032 --> 00:09:02.360
as a game and how that might
be a dangerous concept?

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:05.037
- Sure, first of all, the
reason I called the other book

00:09:05.037 --> 00:09:07.590
Money Master the Game is
that every wealthy person

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:10.737
I interviewed called it
a game, every single one.

00:09:10.737 --> 00:09:13.491
And other people were
saying, that's irresponsible,

00:09:13.491 --> 00:09:14.947
that's horrible to call it a game,

00:09:14.947 --> 00:09:16.401
this is the dumbest thing, that's a,

00:09:16.401 --> 00:09:19.013
but see, that mindset
is what's keeping you

00:09:19.013 --> 00:09:20.650
from investing in the first place.

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:23.208
So, you can be in a game,
it can be a difficult game.

00:09:23.208 --> 00:09:24.543
The challenging games are usually the ones

00:09:24.543 --> 00:09:25.651
that we like the most.

00:09:25.651 --> 00:09:27.766
But this is a game that
can reward your family

00:09:27.766 --> 00:09:29.977
disproportionately to any
other game that's out there.

00:09:29.977 --> 00:09:32.488
Now, some people play games aggressively,

00:09:32.488 --> 00:09:34.774
some play things more in a secure way.

00:09:34.774 --> 00:09:36.512
Your style is more secure.

00:09:36.512 --> 00:09:37.894
And what I teach in this book is

00:09:37.894 --> 00:09:39.659
we want to have a style
that makes you feel good

00:09:39.659 --> 00:09:40.691
'cause you may make a bunch of money

00:09:40.691 --> 00:09:42.926
but you're stressed all the
